 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Decoding",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "A, B and C form a binary address with A as the lowest bit. With the part enabled, the output whose number equals the address is high and the other seven are low."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Enables",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The part is enabled only with G1 high and both G2A and G2B low. Any other enable state holds all eight outputs low whatever the address."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Demultiplexing",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Any one enable can take a data stream. The address then steers that data to the selected output, the other outputs staying low: G1 passes it true, G2A or G2B passes it inverted."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Expansion",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "With one active-high and two active-low enables, decoders can be stacked without extra logic: three make a 24-line decoder with no inverter, four make a 32-line decoder with one inverter."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Memory decoding",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "In a memory system with a fast enable circuit, decoder delay plus memory enable time is usually below the memory's typical access time, so the decoder's added delay is negligible."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inputs",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Input thresholds sit at 30 % and 70 % of the supply, so noise margin is the same at both levels. Every unused input must be tied to VCC or GND; a floating CMOS input has an undefined level and leaves the logic in an undefined state."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Outputs",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Outputs source and sink 24 mA and can drive 15 F-family loads. Propagation delays rise and fall alike. Tested singly, an output is shown to drive a 50 Ω line at 85 °C and a 75 Ω line at 125 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Process",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The CMOS process and circuit design resist SCR latch-up. ESD protection is above 2 kV under MIL-STD-883 method 3015."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply and bypassing",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Any supply within the recommended 1.5 V to 5.5 V range works. VCC takes a 0.1 µF bypass capacitor placed as close to the pin as possible; several capacitors in parallel, commonly 0.1 µF with 1 µF, cover different noise frequencies."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Recommended layout: a 0.1 µF capacitor from VCC (pin 16) to GND (pin 8) next to the package; unused inputs tied to VCC or GND; unused outputs left open; signal traces without 90° corners; spare copper flooded with ground for signal isolation, noise reduction and heat spreading."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal pad",
   "page": 22,
   "text": "On the WQFN (BQB) package the exposed pad is soldered to the board for best thermal and mechanical results. Vias in the pad are optional; vias under paste should be filled, plugged or tented."
  }
